// The Dojo Bay — permission for the service account to restart its own service.
//
// The last step of a self-update is `systemctl restart dojobay-server.service`,
// run as the service account. systemd asks polkit whether that account may
// manage that unit, and the default answer for a system account with no session
// is no. Without this rule an update installs the new code and then stops on its
// final line: new code on disk, old process still serving it, which looks
// exactly like an update that did nothing.
//
// Install (the installer offers to do this for you), substituting your web root:
//   sudo cp /var/www/dojobay/deploy/polkit-restart.rules.example \
//     /etc/polkit-1/rules.d/49-dojobay-restart.rules
// The .example ending is part of this file's name, not a placeholder. The
// destination name is what polkit reads.
//
// 49- so it is read before the distribution's own 50-default.rules. Nothing
// here weakens that default: a rule that returns no result falls through to it.
//
// The four conditions are all of them deliberate. One action, one unit, one
// verb, one account: this grants the right to restart dojobay-server.service
// and nothing else, to the service account and nobody else. It does not permit
// stop, start, enable, mask, or any other unit. An operator who wants to take
// the service down still does it as a human with sudo.
//
// polkit reads .rules files with an internal JavaScript engine (polkit 0.106
// and later, which includes every currently supported distribution). Syntax
// errors are logged by polkitd and the file is skipped, so check
// `journalctl -u polkit` after editing this by hand.
polkit.addRule(function (action, subject) {
  if (action.id == "org.freedesktop.systemd1.manage-units" &&
      action.lookup("unit") == "dojobay-server.service" &&
      action.lookup("verb") == "restart" &&
      subject.user == "dojobay") {
    return polkit.Result.YES;
  }
});
